                        title: "The Verdict — and How to Do It Right",
                        icon: "Map",
                        content: "**Our honest verdict**: Pattaya is worth visiting **as a 2–4 day add-on to Bangkok**, particularly if you have children, a limited budget, or limited time. It is **not** worth structuring a Thailand trip around, and it is not the place to go if you want a beautiful beach.\n\nIf you go, four decisions determine your experience:\n\n**1. Stay in the right neighbourhood.** This matters more than anything else. **Naklua** (north, quiet, Thai, good seafood) or **Jomtien** (long beach, families and long-stayers) for a relaxed trip; **Pratumnak** — the green hill between the two — is the best compromise for most people. **Avoid South Pattaya / Walking Street** unless that's specifically why you came. Our [where to stay in Pattaya guide](/thailand/pattaya/where-to-stay-in-pattaya) breaks each area down.\n\n**2. Get off the mainland.** Spend a day on [Koh Larn](/thailand/pattaya/koh-larn-island-guide) or take a [catamaran to the quieter outer islands](/thailand/pattaya/monkey-island-chonburi-boat-tour). The water improves dramatically the moment you leave the bay.\n\n**3. Do the two genuinely world-class things.** The Sanctuary of Truth and Nong Nooch. Both are inexpensive and both are better than their marketing.\n\n**4. Eat where the Thais eat.** Naklua's seafood restaurants and the local markets, not the beach-road tourist strips.\n\n**Getting there**: buses from Bangkok's Ekkamai terminal run every 30 minutes (about ฿130, 2 hours); minivans from Victory Monument are faster and cheaper; a private car is about ฿1,500–2,000. From Suvarnabhumi Airport it's around 90 minutes, which makes Pattaya a genuinely practical first or last stop on a Thailand trip.\n\n**Best time to go**: **November to February**, when the Gulf is calm and humidity drops. March–May is very hot; the monsoon runs roughly June–October, though Gulf-coast rain is less disruptive than the Andaman's.",
